@* Principle 6 – Users have enough time to read and use the content *@

<h2>Session time limits</h2>
<p>
    Many sites sign you out after a period of inactivity. That's sometimes necessary for security, but users must be
    <strong>warned</strong> before time runs out and allowed to <strong>extend</strong> it (with a simple action, at least
    10 times), and they must not lose their work if it does expire.
</p>
<p>
    This simulation uses a 60-second “session” so you can see the whole cycle. The warning dialog appears with
    20 seconds left. Type something in the box, then let the session expire to see that your text is kept.
</p>

<div class="card" id="session-demo">
    <h3>Simulated session</h3>
    <p>
        Time remaining:
        @* The visible countdown is NOT a live region; announcing it every second would be unbearable.
           Screen reader users get one announcement (the warning dialog) instead. *@
        <span class="timer-display" id="session-time">1:00</span>
    </p>
    <div class="button-row">
        <button type="button" id="session-start">Start the session</button>
        <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="session-off" aria-pressed="false">Turn off the time limit</button>
    </div>

    <div class="form-group">
        <label for="session-draft">Draft a discussion post</label>
        <span class="hint" id="session-draft-hint">Your text is saved in this browser as you type.</span>
        <textarea id="session-draft" aria-describedby="session-draft-hint"></textarea>
    </div>

    <div id="session-expired" class="callout callout-warning" hidden tabindex="-1">
        <p><strong>Your session has expired.</strong> Don't worry: your draft has been saved.</p>
        <button type="button" id="session-reauth">Sign in again</button>
    </div>
</div>

<dialog id="session-warning" aria-labelledby="session-warning-title" aria-describedby="session-warning-desc">
    <h3 id="session-warning-title">Your session is about to expire</h3>
    <p id="session-warning-desc">You will be signed out in about 20 seconds. Do you need more time?</p>
    <div class="button-row">
        <button type="button" id="session-extend">Yes, give me more time</button>
        <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="session-dismiss">No, sign me out</button>
    </div>
</dialog>

<h2>Moving, blinking, and scrolling content</h2>
<p>
    Anything that moves automatically for more than 5 seconds needs a way to pause, stop, or hide it.
    Moving text is hard to read for people who read slowly, and distracting for people with attention disorders.
</p>
<div class="ticker" id="ticker" aria-hidden="true">
    <span class="ticker-track">
        CS Department news: Accessibility lab opens Monday • Hackathon registration now open • Guest lecture on screen readers Thursday at 3 PM •
    </span>
</div>
<p><button type="button" id="ticker-toggle" class="button-secondary">Pause headlines</button></p>
<p>
    The moving ticker is hidden from screen readers (<code>aria-hidden="true"</code>) because the same headlines
    are available as a plain list:
</p>
<ul>
    <li>Accessibility lab opens Monday</li>
    <li>Hackathon registration now open</li>
    <li>Guest lecture on screen readers Thursday at 3 PM</li>
</ul>

<h2>Postpone interruptions</h2>
<p>
    Pop-ups and notifications pull attention away and can be hard to dismiss. Let users postpone non-urgent
    interruptions (emergencies are the exception).
</p>
<div class="card">
    <div class="button-row">
        <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="notify-postpone" aria-pressed="false">Postpone notifications</button>
        <button type="button" id="notify-send">Simulate a new notification</button>
    </div>
    <p role="status" id="notify-status"></p>
    <div class="toast" id="notify-toast" hidden>
        <span id="notify-text"></span>
        <button type="button" class="button-secondary" id="notify-close">Dismiss notification</button>
    </div>
</div>

@section Scripts {
    <script src="~/js/enough-time.js" asp-append-version="true"></script>
}
